+## Domain 4: Peer Art — Phase-as-State-Machine
+
+### Sidekiq (Ruby/Redis)
+- **Model:** job queue with per-worker retry policy; dead-letter queue after N retries
+- **Phase granularity:** none built-in — each "phase" would need to be a separate job class
+- **Resume:** not built-in; requires custom state in Redis/DB
+- **Relevance:** confirms the `retryable + max_attempts + cooldown` pattern in our POLICY_TABLE.
+  Our `dispatch_failure_policy.py` mirrors Sidekiq's `sidekiq_retry_in` / `sidekiq_retries_exhausted` pattern.
+- **Key lesson:** Sidekiq never retries a partially-completed multi-step job from step N — it always restarts. This is exactly the pain STORY-860 fixes.
+
+### Temporal (Go/Java; open-source workflow engine)
+- **Model:** Durable workflows with event-sourced history; each Activity = one unit of work
+- **Phase granularity:** Activity per phase; workflow function loops through activities
+- **Resume:** built-in — replays event history to reconstruct state; activity-level checkpointing
+- **Cancellation:** `context.Context` cancellation propagated to activities; graceful cleanup handlers
+- **Relevance:** closest architectural parallel to what STORY-860 builds:
+  - Workflow = `run_sdlc_phases_v2()`
+  - Activity = `_run_phase_sdk()` call
+  - Heartbeat = `send_heartbeat()` already matches Temporal's `activity.RecordHeartbeat()`
+  - Workflow history = `dispatch_v2_events` append-only log
+- **Key pattern borrowed:** "skip activity if output already exists" = our deliverable-skip resume logic
+- **Key lesson:** Temporal separates workflow state (durable) from activity execution (ephemeral). Our analog: `dispatch_v2_events` is durable state; the SDK subprocess is ephemeral.
+
+### Argo Workflows (Kubernetes/YAML DAG)
+- **Model:** DAG-based; each node = one step; state in etcd via CRDs
+- **Phase granularity:** one node per phase; retry policies per node
+- **Resume:** re-submit from failed node (not from step 1)
+- **Relevance:** confirms phase-as-node / step-as-unit-of-failure granularity. Less relevant for
+  a single-process Python poller, but validates the "emit a typed event per phase failure" pattern.
+- **Key lesson:** Argo emits one event per node (phase) with typed status — maps exactly to
+  AC-4 (`phase_started` / `phase_completed` / `phase_failed` events).
+
+### BullMQ (Node.js/Redis)
+- **Model:** job queue with retry + delayed jobs + parent-child job relationships
+- **Relevance:** parent job = the claim; child jobs = phases. Parent waits for children.
+  Not directly applicable (we want in-process, not separate jobs per phase) but validates
+  the "structured phase output" pattern.
+
+### Synthesis for STORY-860
+| Concern | Industry Pattern | Our Implementation |
+|---|---|---|
+| Phase-as-unit-of-failure | Temporal activity, Argo node | `_run_phase_sdk()` + phase event |
+| Durable state | Temporal history, Argo etcd | `dispatch_v2_events` append log |
+| Resume from phase N | Temporal replay, Argo re-submit | deliverable-exists skip |
+| SIGTERM / cancellation | Temporal cancel signal, Sidekiq `stop!` | STORY-857 `_handle_sigterm()` |
+| Typed failures | Sidekiq `sidekiq_retry_in`, Temporal ApplicationFailure | `dispatch_failure_policy` + new phase-scoped classes |
+| Rework routing | Temporal signal, Argo argo-submit --from | `claim.rework_of` metadata (no regex) |
+
