+
+## Key Findings
+
+### F1 — v1 orchestration is self-contained and liftable
+The core orchestration loop in `run_sdlc_phases()` is ~300 lines of pure Python (no Django ORM,
+no asyncpg) with well-defined seams. All I/O goes through `subprocess.run` and stdlib `urllib.request`.
+It can be extracted into a `_run_sdlc_phases_v2()` function inside `dispatch_poller_v2.py`
+without importing `sdlc_phase_runner.py` (which has v1 API contracts at the top).
+
+### F2 — `_ensure_branch` is the highest-risk lift
+It has 13+ subprocess calls, the `_parse_target_branch`/seed-read path, stale-question cleanup,
+and the Teams notification hook. The 2026-04-18/19 post-mortem burns happened partly because branch
+state was inconsistent before SDK launch. Any approach must preserve its full logic.
+
+### F3 — Phase event types need a schema migration
+The `dispatch_v2_events.event_type` CHECK constraint does not include `phase_started`,
+`phase_completed`, `phase_failed`. This requires migration 059 to add them.
+**Option A:** ALTER TABLE + drop/recreate CHECK constraint (clean, requires downtime or CONCURRENTLY).
+**Option B:** Separate `dispatch_phase_events` table (no constraint change; joins needed).
+The seed prefers adding to the existing table (AC-4 reads "emitted to dispatch_v2_events").
+
+### F4 — `claim.rework_of` is already plumbed end-to-end
+The v2 `_ActiveClaim` already exposes `rework_of` (from `data.get("rework_of")`). STORY-859's
+`_extract_story_branch()` regex is purely compensating for the missing orchestration. With
+STORY-860, we pass `claim.rework_of` directly to `_ensure_branch` — same as v1.
+
+### F5 — SIGTERM interaction requires care at phase boundaries
+STORY-857's `_handle_sigterm()` terminates the SDK proc and exits 0 immediately. This is correct
+for the outer claim/release loop but needs coordination with the new phase loop: if SIGTERM fires
+mid-phase, the phase should be abandoned (SDK killed), the partial work NOT emitted as phase_completed,
+and `release_claim()` called so the job re-queues. The v1 `_shutdown_requested` Event pattern
+(set flag → break after current phase) is the right model, but STORY-857's handler is more abrupt.
+**The v2 orchestrator must set a flag checked between phases; the SIGTERM handler's SDK kill
+remains, but the claim must be released (not transitioned as submitted/failed) if no phase completed.**
+
+### F6 — Heartbeat threading needs v2 adaptation
+v1's `_heartbeat_thread` POSTs to `/api/dispatch/heartbeat` (v1). The v2 equivalent must call
+`send_heartbeat(claim, session=session, headers=headers)` instead. The thread structure is reusable.
+
+### F7 — Session reuse (--resume) requires no change
+`claude_sdk_tool.py` already handles `--resume <sid>`. The v2 orchestrator can read/write the
+same `~/.dispatch-sessions/{story_id}.sid` file that v1 uses. Zero changes needed.
+
